<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johannes Bugenhagen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1485–1558</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Doctor Pomeranus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johannes Bugenhagen was a Pomeranian theologian, close colleague of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> and pastor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, known as Doctor Pomeranus. He wrote church ordinances for many north German cities and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. In his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> he drafted the Lutheran church order adopted after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-treptow">Diet of Treptow</a> in 1534, which brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-bugenhagen.jpg" alt="Johannes Bugenhagen" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucas_Cranach_(I)_-_Johannes_Bugenhagen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
